The `swift test` fails due to sandbox restrictions in this environment, not a code issue. The fix is correct — let me verify the logic against all test cases:

**The bug:** The old code split by space and filtered by `hasPrefix("#")`. This only catches tags that appear at the start of a space-delimited token. Tags embedded after other characters (commas, parens, etc.) were missed.

**The fix:** Replace the space-split filter with a regex `#([\w-]+)` that finds `#` followed by word characters/hyphens anywhere in the text, extracting just the captured group (the tag name without `#`).

**Verification against all cases:**

| Input | Old behavior | New behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `"hello #world #swift"` | `["world", "swift"]` ✓ | `["world", "swift"]` ✓ |
| `"just plain text"` | `[]` ✓ | `[]` ✓ |
| `"shopping list,#urgent."` | `[]` ✗ (no space-delimited token starts with `#`) | `["urgent"]` ✓ |
| `"(#ideas)"` | `[]` ✗ (no space-delimited token starts with `#`) | `["ideas"]` ✓ |

**Changes made:**

- **File:** `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift` (lines 4–12)
- **Before:** Split by space, filter by `hasPrefix("#")`, drop the `#`
- **After:** Regex `#([\w-]+)` finds all `#tag-name` patterns anywhere in the text, extracts the captured group

The fix preserves existing behavior (order of tags, plain text returning `[]`, space-delimited tags still working) while correctly extracting tags embedded after punctuation like `,`, `(`, `)`, etc.


